up the Qwen models' rates to push this to 30.4% and keep the layer gap at a reasonable 3.5 percentage points.\n\nNow I'm finalizing the pooled error type rates across all five models and confirming the Spec versus Ling averages are internally consistent. With the control rates locked in, I can write out the complete YAML configuration.\n\nLet me verify the overall ambiguous rate by averaging across all error types, then compute the binary delta between ambiguous and unambiguous performance. I'm also starting to think through the failure mode layer match percentages and what they represent in the worst tier.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the comprehensive YAML with all these metrics. Before I do, let me consider the implications of the plan_004 analysis—the equivalence testing shows weaker differentiation at tighter margins, and the power analysis reveals the study is underpowered to detect a layer effect given the observed gap is well below the minimum detectable effect. The logistic regression does show a significant interaction between model and type, though the type main effect itself is weak.\n\nFor plan_005, the error analysis reveals that failure modes don't cleanly separate by layer—assumption-based actions and surface scope adoption appear across both layers at similar rates, suggesting the layer distinction isn't as predictive as expected. The Qwen3-8B pattern is interesting because its lower violation rate seems driven by conservative refusal behavior rather than genuine disambiguation ability.\n\nLooking at plan_006 reliability metrics, the overall ICC is moderate at 0.58, with notable variation across types—auth_scope and CP show better agreement while coref is notably lower, and the bootstrap confidence intervals are still being calculated.\n\nFor plan_009, the judge calibration reveals systematic bias: the human annotator achieved κ of 0.68, but the judge shows a concerning pattern of over-counting spec-layer violations while under-counting coref errors, which could artificially inflate the observed layer hierarchy. The per-type F1 scores range from 0.62 to 0.78, with auth_scope, CP, and coref falling below the 0.75 threshold.\n\nThe classifier's binary F1 is solid at 0.83, but per-type performance drops significantly.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how to frame the narrative. The binary effect at +28pp remains the strongest causal signal even with perturbation controls, so the primary reframe emphasizes that ambiguity detection alone—without needing type-specific information—drives massive safety improvements. The secondary angle focuses on how all five models exhibit the same blindness mechanism despite their different type-specific error patterns, which positions this as a universal phenomenon rather than model-specific failure. The paper's core story becomes about how policy ambiguity systematically causes safety violations across LLM agents.\n\nThe three main contributions are the causal quantification of that binary effect, the error analysis revealing the ambiguity blindness mechanism, and the model-dependent vulnerability profiles as supporting evidence. Layer hierarchy gets pushed to supplementary material.\n\nI'm also noticing something important in the inter-model agreement metrics—the Fleiss' κ of 0.62 on ambiguous episodes shows the models are converging on the same wrong interpretation rather than diverging in their readings. This is systematic bias, not true ambiguity in the theoretical sense, which connects back to the Berry & Kamsties framework where nocuity requires reader divergence.\n\nNow I'm organizing the YAML output with the violation rate matrices for each plan, including the per-type breakdowns for plan_002 and plan_003, plus the pooled analysis for plan_004. I need to reconcile how the new plan_002 with Claude added to the original two GPT models shifts the profiles, and how plan_003's open-source models further compress the layer gap, pushing everything into that worst tier.\n\nThe key distinction is that the registry's plan_002 ran on an incomplete clause set with just 2 models, but the new planned run will be the full 300 clauses across 2 conditions with all 3 API models—1,800 episodes total instead of the original 1,200. So I'm predicting results for this expanded version, and the numbers I've calculated should hold.\n\nNow I'm verifying my predictions against the expected ranges: binary effect around 28 percentage points fits the 25-33 range, layer gap at 3.5pp fits 0-8, Cramér's V at 0.11 fits 0.08-0.14, judge-human F1 at 0.71 fits 0.6-0.75, and κ at 0.63 fits 0.55-0.7. The failure mode layer match prediction appears to be cut off, but I'm on track with the other metrics.\n\nFor the per-model breakdown, I'm distributing the pooled 28pp effect across the five models with gpt-5.4 at +33pp down to Qwen3-8B at +20pp, which averages to 27pp and aligns with my overall estimate.\n\nNow I need to examine the model×type interaction effect more carefully to see how the ambiguous condition amplifies violations differently across models. feature of worst tier is that type ranking is model-dependent.\n\nType rankings by model (ambiguous violation rate, highest to lowest):\n\ngpt-5.4: auth_scope(48) > incomp(46) > scopal(38) > lexical(36) > CP(34) > coref(24)\ngpt-4.1: auth_scope(46) > incomp(44) > scopal(36) > lexical(34) > CP(32) > coref(22)\nclaude: incomp(40) > scopal(38) > auth_scope(36) > lexical(34) > coref(34) > CP(30)\nQwen3-32B: auth_scope(38) > incomp(36) > scopal(32) > coref(32) > lexical(30) > CP(28)\nQwen3-8B: coref(40) > scopal(32) > incomp(30) > auth_scope(28) > lexical(26) > CP(24)\n\nThe GPT models show consistent patterns, but Claude reorders incomp and auth_scope while elevating coref, and Qwen3-8B diverges dramatically with coref at the top and auth_scope dropping significantly. Kendall's W across these rankings is around 0.55, indicating moderate agreement but substantial disordinal interactions that make the type hierarchy model-dependent. I need to finalize the YAML with these rankings, making sure Qwen3-8B's coref is set to 40%. my earlier adjustment to 38%). Let me use 40% for Qwen3-8B coref.\n\nRevised 5-model coref: (24+22+34+32+40)/5 = 30.4%\n\nLet me also re-check Qwen3-32B. I had:\nauth_scope 38, incomp 36, scopal 32, lexical 30, CP 28, coref 32\n\nQwen3-32B pooled: (38+36+32+30+28+32)/6 = 32.7% ambiguous\n\nOK that works with the above per-model stats.\n\nLet me now finalize and write. I'll try to include tables in the YAML as embedded strings.\n\nOne more thing for plan_010 perturbation control. In worst tier, it needs to PASS. Let me give specific numbers:\n\n30 perturbation pairs × 2 conditions × 3 API models (including Claude now) = 180 episodes total.\n\nLooking at the perturbation results: the violation rate jumped from 4.7% to 8.3% when perturbed, a difference of 3.6 percentage points. The bootstrap confidence interval spans from -2.8 to 10.0, which contains zero, so this passes. The ratio of perturbation effect to the full study's ambiguity delta is 0.129, well under the 0.15 threshold.\n\nNow moving to plan_001b mini-pilot with 20 episodes across 5 clause types, 2 conditions, and 1 model.\n\nThe coreferential clauses show 60% violation in the ambiguous condition but only 20% in control—that control rate is borderline high and worth flagging. The auth_scope clauses are clearer, hitting 80% ambiguous violations versus 0% control. Both clause types trigger the expected pattern, though I'm concerned the pilot might give false confidence since gpt-5.4 is the strongest model; the real challenges will surface with weaker models. For clause generation itself, quality variations could be a confound—some auth_scope clauses might have unintended differences that Claude handles distinctly, and coref clauses might vary in their structural properties in ways that affect the results.\n\nGenerated 300 clause pairs with reviewers catching 8% quality issues, though most passed after revision. Some subtle confounds likely slipped through that could explain model-specific behavior. Now looking at the realism validation metrics—sentence length and readability look good, but the lexical diversity is significantly lower in synthetic clauses, and auth_scope clauses are structurally simpler than natural ones, so the synthetic set only partially matches the natural distribution.\n\nThe classifier PoC shows strong binary ambiguity detection at 0.83 F1, but the 6-way type classification is weak at 0.52 F1, which aligns with the finding that type boundaries are fuzzy. Before writing up the YAML, I should verify the original plan_002 registry data on auth_scope coverage.\n\nNow I'm looking at how the new plan_002 results shift when adding three API models to the original two GPT models. The API-pooled type rates show auth_scope and incompleteness both dropping to around 43%, with other categories like scopal and lexical in the mid-30s, suggesting Claude and the Qwen models pull the distribution toward the middle compared to the GPT-only baseline.\n\nComputing the API layer gap, I get about 6.6 percentage points—a dramatic drop from the original ~19pp when it was just GPT models. That's a meaningful effect from introducing Claude. For the API-only models with 1800 episodes, I'm estimating V around 0.14, and when pooling all five models across 3000 episodes, V drops to approximately 0.11.\n\nBefore I finalize the presentation, I need to verify the episode counts: plan_002 should be 300 clauses × 2 conditions × 3 API models equaling 1800 episodes, and plan_003 follows a similar structure. per-model rate is based on 50 episodes, so percentages like 48% translate to 24 violations out of 50. Checking my numbers: the ambiguous rates hold up (auth_scope at 48% = 24/50, coref at 40% = 20/50), and the control rates are reasonable too (4% = 2/50 for the best model, 12% = 6/50 for the worst). Now for plan_006, I'm looking at 30 clauses with 3 replicates across 2 conditions and 5 m