d contributions while treating the layer-level claims as exploratory observations.\n\nNow I'm working through the experiment specifications. The clause generation quality is consistent across tiers, though worst-tier confounds in the clauses themselves drive model-specific failures downstream. The mini-pilot with GPT-5.4 shows a clean pattern that creates false confidence—the real issues surface when testing other models. Plan_010 passes but the confidence interval runs slightly wider than preferred. With the three API models, Claude's flat profile across layers collapses the API-only layer gap from roughly 19 percentage points down to 8, while the type hierarchy remains visible in GPT models.\n\nThe open-source models tell a different story—Qwen3-8B reverses the expected pattern with coref performing best, and both local models show weaker binary effects around 22-26 percentage points. Pooling all five models together, the binary effect stabilizes around 30 percentage points with a layer gap of about 5 points, though the interaction between model and type reaches significance and equivalence testing suggests the study lacks power for detecting the layer effect. The error analysis reveals substantial overlap in failure modes and low inter-annotator agreement, making it difficult to cleanly distinguish specification-driven from linguistic mechanisms.\n\nReliability metrics show moderate consistency overall with ICC around 0.58, though coreference judgments are notably less reliable at 0.42. The synthetic data passes most realism checks but shows elevated sentence length and parse depth. Judge calibration analysis reveals systematic biases—the judge over-counts authorization scope violations and under-counts coreference errors, which could compress the corrected layer gap to under 3 percentage points. The binary classifier achieves strong F1 around 0.82, supporting the practical contribution. Now I'm compiling the final YAML with all experiments and verifying the numb